  user_input {
    user_response: "# MediVoice AI \342\200\224 Phase 1: Backend Foundation and Database Design\n\n## Your role\n\nAct as a senior backend engineer and AI systems architect. We are building MediVoice AI, a voice-first hospital operations platform for a fictional hospital named Aarogya One Hospital.\n\nThis is the first implementation phase of a larger project for the Great Indian AI Internship Challenge.\n\nOur immediate objective is to establish a clean backend architecture, define the database schema, create synthetic hospital data, and prepare the system for AI-driven database retrieval and writes.\n\nDo not attempt to build the entire product in this phase. Focus on a reliable foundation that we can test before connecting a reasoning model or speech APIs.\n\n## 1. Product requirements\n\nMediVoice AI will eventually support:\n\n1. Patients conversing naturally with an AI assistant.\n2. Searching doctors and retrieving available appointment slots.\n3. Booking appointments through validated backend tools.\n4. Automatically displaying booked appointments in the selected doctor\'s dashboard.\n5. Allowing doctors to update appointment status and record follow-up recommendations.\n6. Allowing laboratory staff to manage lab-order workflow status.\n7. Detecting overdue lab orders and creating Workflow Intelligence tasks.\n8. Assigning, investigating, and resolving operational exceptions with an audit trail.\n\nFor this phase, focus on database foundations and the first three capabilities needed for appointment retrieval and booking.\n\nUse synthetic demo data only. Do not implement full electronic medical records or clinical-result management.\n\n## 2. Technology stack\n\nBackend:\n- Python\n- FastAPI\n- Pydantic\n- SQLAlchemy 2.x\n- Alembic\n- PostgreSQL\n- Pytest\n\nUse a modular structure with separate database models, API schemas, business services, configuration, routes, and tests.\n\nThe existing frontend may use React, TypeScript, and Vite. Do not replace a working frontend or mix backend logic into React components.\n\nIf the current Google AI Studio environment cannot run a persistent FastAPI server or PostgreSQL database, generate the backend files and instructions clearly. Do not pretend that a real database has been created or connected when it has not.\n\n## 3. Project structure\n\nCreate or adapt this structure:\n\nbackend/\n  app/\n    main.py\n    core/\n      config.py\n    db/\n      base.py\n      session.py\n    models/\n      hospital.py\n      department.py\n      doctor.py\n      appointment_slot.py\n      appointment.py\n      lab_order.py\n      workflow_exception.py\n      audit_event.py\n    schemas/\n      hospital.py\n      doctor.py\n      appointment.py\n      lab_order.py\n      workflow.py\n    services/\n      doctor_service.py\n      appointment_service.py\n      lab_service.py\n      workflow_service.py\n    api/\n      v1/\n        router.py\n        hospitals.py\n        doctors.py\n        appointments.py\n        lab_orders.py\n        workflow.py\n  tests/\n    test_doctors.py\n    test_appointments.py\n    test_lab_orders.py\n    test_workflow.py\n  alembic/\n  requirements.txt\n  .env.example\n  README.md\n\nKeep the implementation appropriately small for the first phase. Create files only when they are needed, but maintain clear separation of concerns.\n\n## 4. Database schema\n\nUse PostgreSQL-compatible SQLAlchemy models with UUID primary keys where practical, timestamps, foreign keys, appropriate indexes, and explicit status fields.\n\n### Hospital\nFields:\n- id\n- name\n- description\n- address\n- is_active\n- created_at\n\nSeed the fictional Aarogya One Hospital.\n\n### Department\nFields:\n- id\n- hospital_id\n- name\n- description\n- is_active\n\nSeed departments such as Cardiology, Neurology, Orthopedics, Pediatrics, and General Medicine.\n\n### Doctor\nFields:\n- id\n- hospital_id\n- department_id\n- display_name\n- specialty\n- is_active\n\nUse fictional doctor identities and synthetic information.\n\n### AppointmentSlot\nFields:\n- id\n- doctor_id\n- start_time\n- end_time\n- status\n- created_at\n\nRepresent appointment availability explicitly. Prevent two appointments from reserving the same slot. Use an appropriate database constraint and transaction-safe booking logic.\n\n### Appointment\nFields:\n- id\n- patient_reference\n- doctor_id\n- slot_id\n- appointment_status\n- reason_category\n- created_at\n- updated_at\n- visit_completed_at\n- follow_up_recommended\n- follow_up_date\n- follow_up_notes\n\nUse synthetic patient references rather than real personal information. Keep follow-up notes operational and avoid implementing clinical records.\n\nUse appropriate relationships and indexes. Ensure cancelled appointments and slot availability are handled consistently.\n\n### LabOrder\nFields:\n- id\n- patient_reference\n- ordering_department_id\n- test_category\n- workflow_status\n- expected_completion_at\n- assigned_team\n- delay_reason\n- created_at\n- updated_at\n\nAllowed workflow statuses:\n- Pending\n- Processing\n- Completed\n- Delayed\n\nThis model represents workflow metadata only, not clinical results.\n\n### WorkflowException\nFields:\n- id\n- exception_type\n- title\n- description\n- priority\n- responsible_department\n- assigned_to\n- status\n- affected_record_type\n- affected_record_id\n- detected_at\n- updated_at\n- resolved_at\n- resolution_note\n\nUse explicit enumerated values or validated literals for priority and status.\n\n### AuditEvent\nFields:\n- id\n- actor_reference\n- action\n- entity_type\n- entity_id\n- details\n- created_at\n\nAudit events must be created when important booking, status, assignment, or resolution operations occur.\n\n## 5. Initial API endpoints\n\nImplement these first.\n\n### Hospital and doctors\n\nGET /api/v1/hospitals\n\nGET /api/v1/hospitals/{hospital_id}\n\nGET /api/v1/doctors\n\nSupport optional filters such as specialty, department, and hospital.\n\nGET /api/v1/doctors/{doctor_id}\n\nGET /api/v1/doctors/{doctor_id}/slots\n\nAllow filtering available slots by date and time range.\n\nReturn structured JSON using Pydantic response schemas. Validate inputs and use appropriate HTTP status codes.\n\n### Appointments\n\nPOST /api/v1/appointments\n\nRequest fields:\n- patient_reference\n- doctor_id\n- slot_id\n- reason_category\n\nValidate the selected doctor and slot, verify that the slot is still available, and reserve it using a database transaction.\n\nDo not accept an arbitrary appointment status from the patient. The server controls the initial status.\n\nGET /api/v1/appointments/{appointment_id}\n\nGET /api/v1/appointments\n\nSupport filtering by doctor, date, and appointment status as appropriate.\n\nPOST /api/v1/appointments/{appointment_id}/cancel\n\nUse a controlled cancellation operation and update slot availability consistently.\n\nPOST /api/v1/appointments/{appointment_id}/complete\n\nMark the operational visit as completed. Do not accept fabricated clinical notes.\n\nPOST /api/v1/appointments/{appointment_id}/follow-up\n\nRecord a doctor-recommended follow-up date and operational note. Do not automatically create or confirm the next appointment. A separate appointment booking must use the normal availability and confirmation workflow.\n\n## 6. Booking reliability\n\nAppointment creation is a critical operation.\n\nImplement:\n- Transaction-safe slot reservation.\n- Database-level protection against double booking.\n- Validation that the selected doctor owns the selected slot.\n- Validation that the slot is in the future and available.\n- Appropriate rollback when booking fails.\n- Correct HTTP errors for unavailable or invalid slots.\n- Audit events for successful booking and cancellation.\n- Protection against duplicate booking requests where practical.\n\nNever return a successful booking response until the database transaction has committed.\n\nIf simultaneous requests target the same slot, only one should succeed.\n\n## 7. Synthetic seed data\n\nProvide a repeatable development seed script that creates:\n- One fictional hospital.\n- Several departments.\n- At least six fictional doctors across different departments.\n- Multiple future appointment slots per doctor.\n- Several synthetic appointments in different statuses.\n- Several synthetic lab orders, including an overdue Processing order.\n- A small set of workflow exceptions and audit events.\n\nThe seed script should avoid creating duplicate records when rerun, or clearly document how to reset the demo database.\n\nUse fixed, predictable synthetic records for repeatable tests.\n\n## 8. Configuration and database connection\n\nUse environment variables for database configuration.\n\nCreate .env.example with placeholder values only.\n\nRequirements:\n- Never hardcode real credentials.\n- Never expose secrets in frontend code or version control.\n- Use SQLAlchemy session dependencies with proper cleanup.\n- Use Alembic for database migrations.\n- Provide commands for creating and applying migrations.\n- Provide a safe development database setup guide.\n\nDo not assume PostgreSQL is already available. Clearly document whether the database connection has actually been tested.\n\n## 9. Initial AI tool readiness\n\nDo not integrate an external model yet.\n\nPrepare typed Python service functions with clear input and output schemas for these future AI tools:\n\n### search_doctors\nInputs:\n- specialty or department\n- optional hospital identifier\n\nOutput:\n- matching doctor identifiers\n- display names\n- specialties\n- department names\n\n### get_available_slots\nInputs:\n- doctor identifier\n- requested date\n- optional time range\n\nOutput:\n- currently available slot identifiers\n- start and end times\n- relevant doctor details\n\n### book_appointment\nInputs:\n- patient reference\n- doctor identifier\n- selected slot identifier\n- reason category\n\nOutput:\n- confirmed appointment identifier\n- doctor details\n- appointment time\n- booking status\n\nThe eventual AI orchestrator must invoke these tools through a restricted allowlist. The model must never execute SQL, directly access the database, or choose arbitrary functions.\n\nThe backend must validate every tool input independently of the model.\n\nPatient confirmation must occur before the booking tool is invoked. Do not treat model-generated text as proof that a patient confirmed.\n\n## 10. Workflow Intelligence preparation\n\nDo not implement the full workflow engine yet.\n\nPrepare the schema and service boundaries so that a later phase can:\n- Detect lab orders exceeding their expected turnaround.\n- Create exceptions without duplicate open tasks for the same underlying issue.\n- Assign an exception to the appropriate team.\n- Record investigation notes.\n- Resolve an exception only after the underlying operational record has been checked.\n- Preserve an audit trail.\n\nUse deterministic business rules for detection. The model may explain or summarize exceptions later, but it must not invent operational facts.\n\n## 11. Testing requirements\n\nWrite tests for:\n- Doctor retrieval.\n- Filtering doctors by specialty or department.\n- Available-slot retrieval.\n- Successful appointment booking.\n- Rejection of unavailable slots.\n- Prevention of double booking.\n- Validation of doctor-slot ownership.\n- Appointment cancellation and slot release.\n- Appointment completion.\n- Follow-up recommendation recording.\n- Audit event creation.\n\nUse an isolated test database or suitable transaction-based test fixtures. Do not run destructive tests against a production database.\n\n## 12. API documentation and verification\n\nEnable FastAPI OpenAPI documentation at /docs.\n\nProvide:\n- Installation instructions.\n- Environment setup.\n- Database creation instructions.\n- Migration commands.\n- Seed commands.\n- Server startup command.\n- Test commands.\n- Example requests and responses.\n- A clear list of implemented and unimplemented features.\n\nIf the current environment supports execution, run the available tests and fix errors. Otherwise, provide exact local commands for verification and state honestly what has not been executed.\n\n## 13. Strict scope boundaries\n\nFor this phase:\n- No live Gnani Prisma speech-to-text integration.\n- No live Timbre text-to-speech integration.\n- No Evon model integration.\n- No model API keys or unnecessary credit usage.\n- No unrestricted AI database access.\n- No full electronic medical records.\n- No clinical-result creation, editing, or approval.\n- No production authentication claims.\n- Synthetic patient data only.\n\n## 14. Acceptance criteria\n\nThe phase is complete only when:\n1. The project has a clean modular backend structure.\n2. SQLAlchemy models and relationships are defined.\n3. PostgreSQL configuration and migrations are prepared.\n4. Synthetic seed data can be loaded.\n5. Doctor and slot retrieval endpoints are implemented.\n6. Appointment booking is transaction-safe.\n7. The initial tool-service interfaces are ready for AI integration.\n8. Tests cover the critical booking behavior.\n9. API documentation and setup instructions are provided.\n10. The implementation clearly distinguishes code generated from code actually executed and tested.\n\nStart by inspecting the current project. Preserve existing working files, implement this phase incrementally, and summarize the files created, commands to run, tests performed, and remaining blockers. Do not proceed to later phases until this foundation is verified."
    items {
      text: "# MediVoice AI \342\200\224 Phase 1: Backend Foundation and Database Design\n\n## Your role\n\nAct as a senior backend engineer and AI systems architect. We are building MediVoice AI, a voice-first hospital operations platform for a fictional hospital named Aarogya One Hospital.\n\nThis is the first implementation phase of a larger project for the Great Indian AI Internship Challenge.\n\nOur immediate objective is to establish a clean backend architecture, define the database schema, create synthetic hospital data, and prepare the system for AI-driven database retrieval and writes.\n\nDo not attempt to build the entire product in this phase. Focus on a reliable foundation that we can test before connecting a reasoning model or speech APIs.\n\n## 1. Product requirements\n\nMediVoice AI will eventually support:\n\n1. Patients conversing naturally with an AI assistant.\n2. Searching doctors and retrieving available appointment slots.\n3. Booking appointments through validated backend tools.\n4. Automatically displaying booked appointments in the selected doctor\'s dashboard.\n5. Allowing doctors to update appointment status and record follow-up recommendations.\n6. Allowing laboratory staff to manage lab-order workflow status.\n7. Detecting overdue lab orders and creating Workflow Intelligence tasks.\n8. Assigning, investigating, and resolving operational exceptions with an audit trail.\n\nFor this phase, focus on database foundations and the first three capabilities needed for appointment retrieval and booking.\n\nUse synthetic demo data only. Do not implement full electronic medical records or clinical-result management.\n\n## 2. Technology stack\n\nBackend:\n- Python\n- FastAPI\n- Pydantic\n- SQLAlchemy 2.x\n- Alembic\n- PostgreSQL\n- Pytest\n\nUse a modular structure with separate database models, API schemas, business services, configuration, routes, and tests.\n\nThe existing frontend may use React, TypeScript, and Vite. Do not replace a working frontend or mix backend logic into React components.\n\nIf the current Google AI Studio environment cannot run a persistent FastAPI server or PostgreSQL database, generate the backend files and instructions clearly. Do not pretend that a real database has been created or connected when it has not.\n\n## 3. Project structure\n\nCreate or adapt this structure:\n\nbackend/\n  app/\n    main.py\n    core/\n      config.py\n    db/\n      base.py\n      session.py\n    models/\n      hospital.py\n      department.py\n      doctor.py\n      appointment_slot.py\n      appointment.py\n      lab_order.py\n      workflow_exception.py\n      audit_event.py\n    schemas/\n      hospital.py\n      doctor.py\n      appointment.py\n      lab_order.py\n      workflow.py\n    services/\n      doctor_service.py\n      appointment_service.py\n      lab_service.py\n      workflow_service.py\n    api/\n      v1/\n        router.py\n        hospitals.py\n        doctors.py\n        appointments.py\n        lab_orders.py\n        workflow.py\n  tests/\n    test_doctors.py\n    test_appointments.py\n    test_lab_orders.py\n    test_workflow.py\n  alembic/\n  requirements.txt\n  .env.example\n  README.md\n\nKeep the implementation appropriately small for the first phase. Create files only when they are needed, but maintain clear separation of concerns.\n\n## 4. Database schema\n\nUse PostgreSQL-compatible SQLAlchemy models with UUID primary keys where practical, timestamps, foreign keys, appropriate indexes, and explicit status fields.\n\n### Hospital\nFields:\n- id\n- name\n- description\n- address\n- is_active\n- created_at\n\nSeed the fictional Aarogya One Hospital.\n\n### Department\nFields:\n- id\n- hospital_id\n- name\n- description\n- is_active\n\nSeed departments such as Cardiology, Neurology, Orthopedics, Pediatrics, and General Medicine.\n\n### Doctor\nFields:\n- id\n- hospital_id\n- department_id\n- display_name\n- specialty\n- is_active\n\nUse fictional doctor identities and synthetic information.\n\n### AppointmentSlot\nFields:\n- id\n- doctor_id\n- start_time\n- end_time\n- status\n- created_at\n\nRepresent appointment availability explicitly. Prevent two appointments from reserving the same slot. Use an appropriate database constraint and transaction-safe booking logic.\n\n### Appointment\nFields:\n- id\n- patient_reference\n- doctor_id\n- slot_id\n- appointment_status\n- reason_category\n- created_at\n- updated_at\n- visit_completed_at\n- follow_up_recommended\n- follow_up_date\n- follow_up_notes\n\nUse synthetic patient references rather than real personal information. Keep follow-up notes operational and avoid implementing clinical records.\n\nUse appropriate relationships and indexes. Ensure cancelled appointments and slot availability are handled consistently.\n\n### LabOrder\nFields:\n- id\n- patient_reference\n- ordering_department_id\n- test_category\n- workflow_status\n- expected_completion_at\n- assigned_team\n- delay_reason\n- created_at\n- updated_at\n\nAllowed workflow statuses:\n- Pending\n- Processing\n- Completed\n- Delayed\n\nThis model represents workflow metadata only, not clinical results.\n\n### WorkflowException\nFields:\n- id\n- exception_type\n- title\n- description\n- priority\n- responsible_department\n- assigned_to\n- status\n- affected_record_type\n- affected_record_id\n- detected_at\n- updated_at\n- resolved_at\n- resolution_note\n\nUse explicit enumerated values or validated literals for priority and status.\n\n### AuditEvent\nFields:\n- id\n- actor_reference\n- action\n- entity_type\n- entity_id\n- details\n- created_at\n\nAudit events must be created when important booking, status, assignment, or resolution operations occur.\n\n## 5. Initial API endpoints\n\nImplement these first.\n\n### Hospital and doctors\n\nGET /api/v1/hospitals\n\nGET /api/v1/hospitals/{hospital_id}\n\nGET /api/v1/doctors\n\nSupport optional filters such as specialty, department, and hospital.\n\nGET /api/v1/doctors/{doctor_id}\n\nGET /api/v1/doctors/{doctor_id}/slots\n\nAllow filtering available slots by date and time range.\n\nReturn structured JSON using Pydantic response schemas. Validate inputs and use appropriate HTTP status codes.\n\n### Appointments\n\nPOST /api/v1/appointments\n\nRequest fields:\n- patient_reference\n- doctor_id\n- slot_id\n- reason_category\n\nValidate the selected doctor and slot, verify that the slot is still available, and reserve it using a database transaction.\n\nDo not accept an arbitrary appointment status from the patient. The server controls the initial status.\n\nGET /api/v1/appointments/{appointment_id}\n\nGET /api/v1/appointments\n\nSupport filtering by doctor, date, and appointment status as appropriate.\n\nPOST /api/v1/appointments/{appointment_id}/cancel\n\nUse a controlled cancellation operation and update slot availability consistently.\n\nPOST /api/v1/appointments/{appointment_id}/complete\n\nMark the operational visit as completed. Do not accept fabricated clinical notes.\n\nPOST /api/v1/appointments/{appointment_id}/follow-up\n\nRecord a doctor-recommended follow-up date and operational note. Do not automatically create or confirm the next appointment. A separate appointment booking must use the normal availability and confirmation workflow.\n\n## 6. Booking reliability\n\nAppointment creation is a critical operation.\n\nImplement:\n- Transaction-safe slot reservation.\n- Database-level protection against double booking.\n- Validation that the selected doctor owns the selected slot.\n- Validation that the slot is in the future and available.\n- Appropriate rollback when booking fails.\n- Correct HTTP errors for unavailable or invalid slots.\n- Audit events for successful booking and cancellation.\n- Protection against duplicate booking requests where practical.\n\nNever return a successful booking response until the database transaction has committed.\n\nIf simultaneous requests target the same slot, only one should succeed.\n\n## 7. Synthetic seed data\n\nProvide a repeatable development seed script that creates:\n- One fictional hospital.\n- Several departments.\n- At least six fictional doctors across different departments.\n- Multiple future appointment slots per doctor.\n- Several synthetic appointments in different statuses.\n- Several synthetic lab orders, including an overdue Processing order.\n- A small set of workflow exceptions and audit events.\n\nThe seed script should avoid creating duplicate records when rerun, or clearly document how to reset the demo database.\n\nUse fixed, predictable synthetic records for repeatable tests.\n\n## 8. Configuration and database connection\n\nUse environment variables for database configuration.\n\nCreate .env.example with placeholder values only.\n\nRequirements:\n- Never hardcode real credentials.\n- Never expose secrets in frontend code or version control.\n- Use SQLAlchemy session dependencies with proper cleanup.\n- Use Alembic for database migrations.\n- Provide commands for creating and applying migrations.\n- Provide a safe development database setup guide.\n\nDo not assume PostgreSQL is already available. Clearly document whether the database connection has actually been tested.\n\n## 9. Initial AI tool readiness\n\nDo not integrate an external model yet.\n\nPrepare typed Python service functions with clear input and output schemas for these future AI tools:\n\n### search_doctors\nInputs:\n- specialty or department\n- optional hospital identifier\n\nOutput:\n- matching doctor identifiers\n- display names\n- specialties\n- department names\n\n### get_available_slots\nInputs:\n- doctor identifier\n- requested date\n- optional time range\n\nOutput:\n- currently available slot identifiers\n- start and end times\n- relevant doctor details\n\n### book_appointment\nInputs:\n- patient reference\n- doctor identifier\n- selected slot identifier\n- reason category\n\nOutput:\n- confirmed appointment identifier\n- doctor details\n- appointment time\n- booking status\n\nThe eventual AI orchestrator must invoke these tools through a restricted allowlist. The model must never execute SQL, directly access the database, or choose arbitrary functions.\n\nThe backend must validate every tool input independently of the model.\n\nPatient confirmation must occur before the booking tool is invoked. Do not treat model-generated text as proof that a patient confirmed.\n\n## 10. Workflow Intelligence preparation\n\nDo not implement the full workflow engine yet.\n\nPrepare the schema and service boundaries so that a later phase can:\n- Detect lab orders exceeding their expected turnaround.\n- Create exceptions without duplicate open tasks for the same underlying issue.\n- Assign an exception to the appropriate team.\n- Record investigation notes.\n- Resolve an exception only after the underlying operational record has been checked.\n- Preserve an audit trail.\n\nUse deterministic business rules for detection. The model may explain or summarize exceptions later, but it must not invent operational facts.\n\n## 11. Testing requirements\n\nWrite tests for:\n- Doctor retrieval.\n- Filtering doctors by specialty or department.\n- Available-slot retrieval.\n- Successful appointment booking.\n- Rejection of unavailable slots.\n- Prevention of double booking.\n- Validation of doctor-slot ownership.\n- Appointment cancellation and slot release.\n- Appointment completion.\n- Follow-up recommendation recording.\n- Audit event creation.\n\nUse an isolated test database or suitable transaction-based test fixtures. Do not run destructive tests against a production database.\n\n## 12. API documentation and verification\n\nEnable FastAPI OpenAPI documentation at /docs.\n\nProvide:\n- Installation instructions.\n- Environment setup.\n- Database creation instructions.\n- Migration commands.\n- Seed commands.\n- Server startup command.\n- Test commands.\n- Example requests and responses.\n- A clear list of implemented and unimplemented features.\n\nIf the current environment supports execution, run the available tests and fix errors. Otherwise, provide exact local commands for verification and state honestly what has not been executed.\n\n## 13. Strict scope boundaries\n\nFor this phase:\n- No live Gnani Prisma speech-to-text integration.\n- No live Timbre text-to-speech integration.\n- No Evon model integration.\n- No model API keys or unnecessary credit usage.\n- No unrestricted AI database access.\n- No full electronic medical records.\n- No clinical-result creation, editing, or approval.\n- No production authentication claims.\n- Synthetic patient data only.\n\n## 14. Acceptance criteria\n\nThe phase is complete only when:\n1. The project has a clean modular backend structure.\n2. SQLAlchemy models and relationships are defined.\n3. PostgreSQL configuration and migrations are prepared.\n4. Synthetic seed data can be loaded.\n5. Doctor and slot retrieval endpoints are implemented.\n6. Appointment booking is transaction-safe.\n7. The initial tool-service interfaces are ready for AI integration.\n8. Tests cover the critical booking behavior.\n9. API documentation and setup instructions are provided.\n10. The implementation clearly distinguishes code generated from code actually executed and tested.\n\nStart by inspecting the current project. Preserve existing working files, implement this phase incrementally, and summarize the files created, commands to run, tests performed, and remaining blockers. Do not proceed to later phases until this foundation is verified."
    }
  }
}
